Changes from previous releases
==============================

Unreleased
----------
The main change is a rework of the oxley domain: its meshes, assembly and MPI
support are rebuilt, local refinement works through a queue that returns a
new domain, and refined meshes are solved on by exporting them to finley.
Much of the oxley API changes; see "oxley: migrating from 6.1.1" below.

Highlights
~~~~~~~~~~
 - oxley: block-structured meshes with a per-block refinement level,
   assembly rewritten and checked against ripley in 2D and 3D, full MPI
   support, local refinement with ``RefinementQueue2D``/``RefinementQueue3D``,
   and an export to finley (``toFinley()``) to solve PDEs on refined meshes.
 - New user guide chapter on oxley, with refinement examples in 2D, 3D and on
   MPI sub-communicators.
 - The bundled p4est/libsc is upgraded from 2.2 to 2.8.7.
 - ``run-escript`` works with OpenMPI 5.
 - macOS: Python extensions no longer link libpython, which made
   ``import esys.escript`` segfault with conda-forge's Boost.

oxley: migrating from 6.1.1
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - ``Rectangle``/``Brick`` take the number of blocks per axis (``n0``,
   ``n1``, ``n2``) and a ``refine_level``, an integer or an array with a level
   per block, by which every block is subdivided.  With ``refine_level=0``
   (the default) a mesh is the same as before.  ``Block(numBlocks, length,
   origin, refine_level, ...)`` is a dimension-independent alternative.
 - The arguments ``d0``/``d1``/``d2``, ``periodic0``/``1``/``2`` and
   ``order`` never had any effect in oxley.  They are accepted and ignored
   with a ``DeprecationWarning``; an unknown keyword argument now raises
   ``TypeError`` instead of being ignored.
 - A domain no longer refines itself.  ``refineMesh``, ``setRefinementLevel``,
   ``refineBoundary``, ``refineRegion``, ``refinePoint``, ``refineCircle``,
   ``refineSphere``, ``applyRefinementZone``, ``setAdaptiveRefinement``,
   ``updateMeshInformation`` and ``updateSolutionInformation`` are gone from
   the domain.  A domain refined in place left the Data defined on it sized
   for a mesh that no longer existed.
 - ``RefinementZone2D``/``RefinementZone3D`` are renamed
   ``RefinementQueue2D``/``RefinementQueue3D``.  ``queue.apply(domain)``
   returns a new, refined domain and leaves ``domain`` and the Data on it
   unchanged.  ``refineUniform(level)`` replaces ``refineMesh("uniform")``,
   and ``refineBorder`` takes the name of the border.
 - A refinement level is absolute: elements are subdivided until they reach
   it.  ``refineUniform(n)`` gives the same mesh as ``refine_level=n``; the
   old ``refineMesh("uniform")`` subdivided once more than asked.
 - ``refineMask`` takes the name of a mask, and the mask is passed to
   ``apply``: ``queue.refineMask("fault", level)`` and then
   ``queue.apply(domain, fault=mask)``.
 - ``loadMesh(filename)`` is a module-level function returning a new domain,
   rather than a domain method replacing its mesh.  ``saveMesh`` writes a
   header ``<filename>.oxley`` with the geometry next to the p4est files, and
   ``loadMesh`` refuses a mesh without one, so meshes saved with 6.1.1 cannot
   be loaded.
 - A ``LinearPDE`` on an oxley domain with hanging nodes (a refined or graded
   mesh) raises ``NotImplementedError``: the oxley assembler does not handle
   hanging nodes and returned a wrong solution without warning.  Solve on the
   finley export instead (see below).  Uniformly refined domains are
   unaffected.

oxley: local refinement
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - ``refineCircle`` and ``refineSphere`` refined nothing: an element was
   refined only if its centre lay exactly on the circle or sphere.  They now
   refine every element that overlaps the disc or ball.
 - ``refineBorder`` refined the wrong elements as soon as the domain was not
   the unit square (nothing, the whole domain, or an arbitrary part of it),
   and in 3D every border raised an error.  A border now refines exactly the
   elements reaching into the strip of width ``dx`` along its face.  In 3D,
   ``"top"``/``"bottom"`` are the faces normal to x2, ``"north"``/``"south"``
   (or ``"back"``/``"front"``) those normal to x1, and ``"east"``/``"west"``
   (or ``"right"``/``"left"``) those normal to x0.
 - ``refineMask`` had no effect.  An element is now refined where the mask is
   positive at any of its quadrature points.  The result does not depend on
   the number of MPI ranks, and a mask refinement may appear anywhere in the
   queue.
 - In 3D, ``refineRegion``, ``refinePoint``, ``refineSphere`` and
   ``refineBorder`` crashed or refined nothing, as the callbacks read the
   wrong tree and forest data.
 - Applying a 3D queue discarded any refinement the ``Brick`` already had,
   since the domain was rebuilt from level 0.  The refinement is now kept.
 - A refined domain no longer depends on its source domain staying alive.

oxley: solving PDEs on refined meshes through finley
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - ``domain.toFinley()`` returns a finley domain with the same mesh, each
   element split into triangles (2D) or tetrahedra (3D), hanging nodes
   included.  It works on graded and refined meshes in 2D and 3D, on any
   number of MPI ranks, and gives the same mesh whatever the number of ranks.
   Its conformity is checked, across ranks too.  ``esys.finley`` has to be
   imported for it to return a usable domain.
 - Tag names, node tags and Dirac points carry over to the export.
 - ``toFinleyData``/``fromFinleyData`` copy a ``ContinuousFunction`` between
   an oxley domain and its export; ``toFinleyReducedData``/
   ``fromFinleyReducedData``, ``toFinleyFunctionData``/
   ``fromFinleyFunctionData`` and ``toFinleyBoundaryData``/
   ``fromFinleyBoundaryData`` do the same for ``ReducedFunction``,
   ``Function`` and ``FunctionOnBoundary``.  Complex Data is supported.
 - oxley builds without Trilinos.  It then has no assembler of its own, and
   PDEs are solved on the finley export.

oxley: assembly and data
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - Nodes are numbered by p4est's ``lnodes`` instead of a hash of their
   coordinates, and the Z/IZ hanging-node condensation is removed.
   ``getMeshInfo()`` gives a Python view of the mesh.
 - 3D results were wrong in many places and now match ripley: an OpenMP race
   in the 3D assembly dropped contributions, only 4 of the 8 corners of an
   element were assembled, elements at the finest level integrated to zero,
   the Gauss points were ordered inconsistently, ``grad()`` returned garbage,
   ``integrate()`` returned 0, ``FunctionOnBoundary`` was not available, PDE
   systems (e.g. elasticity) gave a singular matrix, the Neumann/Robin terms
   were read from the wrong samples, and Dirac points were ignored.
 - ``grad()``, the interpolation onto elements and ``getX()`` take the
   hanging nodes of graded meshes into account, in 2D and 3D and under MPI.

oxley: MPI
~~~~~~~~~~
 - Each rank assembles complete rows for the nodes it owns, including the
   contributions of elements on neighbouring ranks, for single PDEs, systems,
   reduced order and boundary terms, in 2D and 3D.  Results are the same as in
   serial.
 - The mesh no longer depends on the number of ranks (more ranks used to
   refine it further).
 - A Dirac point is placed on one rank only; a point source used to scale
   with the number of ranks.
 - Fixes for boundary faces at partition boundaries, which were dropped,
   ``setToNormal`` on the z faces of a ``Brick``, ``randomFill`` (which
   corrupted the heap) and ``saveDataCSV`` (which aborted).

weipa
~~~~~
 - ``saveVTK``/``saveSilo`` work for 3D oxley domains; they failed or
   crashed before.
 - Under MPI, ``saveVTK`` of a 2D oxley domain no longer drops cells.
 - oxley meshes with hanging nodes, and their face elements, are written with
   every cell in its place, through oxley's new mesh-access interface.

Solvers and Trilinos
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - Serialise ``Tpetra::sumIntoLocalValues`` in the unrolled block matrix
   wrapper used for PDE systems as well.  With OpenMP threads, finley PDE
   systems with Trilinos could abort with "double free or corruption".

Build system and platforms
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - Bundled p4est/libsc upgraded from 2.2 to 2.8.7.
 - macOS: Python extensions resolve the Python C API from the interpreter
   (``-undefined dynamic_lookup``) instead of linking libpython; the duplicate
   libpython corrupted Boost.Python's converter registry at import.
 - ``run-escript`` passes ``--bind-to`` to OpenMPI.  OpenMPI 5 rejects the
   old ``-bind-to``, so every MPI launch failed.
 - Ubuntu 25.04 and later have no ``libparmetis-dev``; the debian/ubuntu
   option templates use the ParMETIS library of ``libscotchparmetis-dev``
   when needed, and the installation guide says so.
 - Debian packaging (``localdebian/``) updated to the 6.1.1-1 packaging,
   restricted to 64-bit architectures.
 - CI workflows moved off the deprecated Node 20 actions.

Testing and examples
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - A test suite from which no tests are collected now fails instead of
   passing silently; five suites were in that state.
 - Examples that need several MPI ranks are run with that many by
   ``scons py_tests``; the weipa tests run again with MPI enabled.
 - ``MT_master_slave.py`` uses a direct solver; the iterative one did not
   converge at the lowest frequency.

Documentation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - New user guide chapter on oxley: meshes, local refinement with
   ``RefinementQueue2D``/``RefinementQueue3D``, refinement driven by a mask,
   solving PDEs through finley, and refinement under MPI and on
   sub-communicators.
 - New examples ``refinement_queue.py``, ``refinement_queue_3d.py`` and
   ``refinement_queue_mpi4py.py``, which write their results to ``data/``.
 - The ``grad()`` docstring says what a gradient on ``FunctionOnBoundary``
   needs from the mesh.
 - ``speckley.tex`` builds with strict TeX Live (Debian #1122465).
 - README updated (conda-forge 6.1.1 for Linux, macOS and Windows).

6.1.1
-----
A maintenance release on top of 6.1.0, focused on build and packaging
robustness across Windows (MSVC), macOS, conda-forge and pip.  There are no
API or numerical changes.

Highlights
~~~~~~~~~~
 - esys-escript now compiles cleanly with the Windows MSVC toolchain.
 - macOS shared libraries and Python extensions carry an RPATH, so
   ``import esys.escript`` works from a plain interpreter without
   ``run-escript``.
 - conda-forge and pip packaging fixes.
 - The ``run-escript`` man page is consistent with the launcher's options.

Windows / MSVC build
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - Use ``&&`` instead of the ``and`` keyword, ``unsigned int`` instead of the
   POSIX ``uint``, signed loop counters for OpenMP ``for`` loops, and an
   explicit ``std::complex<double>`` where MSVC requires it.
 - Guard POSIX-only calls in ``Utils.cpp`` behind ``_WIN32``.
 - Do not propagate ``ESCRIPT_EXPORTS`` to the Python wrapper on Windows;
   skip ``pythonMPIcpp.pyd`` when mpi4py is disabled.
 - Probe for the MUMPS MPI shim header on Windows and ``#undef
   MPI_COMM_WORLD`` before including it.
 - Fix MSVC narrowing warnings; this also uncovered a latent Paso bug, where
   the ``Options::reordering`` field was ``bool`` but must be ``index_t``.

macOS
~~~~~
 - Bake ``<prefix>/lib/esys`` (and ``<prefix>/lib``) as an RPATH into every
   shared library and extension, so bare imports and the SCons configure
   probes resolve ``libomp``/``libgomp`` without ``LD_LIBRARY_PATH``.
   (``SHLINKFLAGS`` rpath dropped as ``LINKFLAGS`` already covers it.)
 - Fix an OpenMP duplicate-runtime abort in Homebrew builds.
 - Drop the deprecated ``boost::math::complex::acos`` fallback.

Packaging
~~~~~~~~~
 - pip: only run the lib-path bootstrap when the ``esys_escript_lib/``
   directory is present, so bare ``import esys`` works for conda and system
   installs.
 - conda-forge recipe updated for the 6.1 series.

Build system
~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - More robust Boost.NumPy detection: find NumPy's include directory early,
   normalise ``boost_libs`` before concatenation, and surface the probe
   exception text on failure.
 - Use raw strings for the gmsh GEO templates; split ``ld_extra`` into
   separate link arguments; generate ``run_escript.py`` before the escript
   SConscript runs.

Other fixes
~~~~~~~~~~~
 - Fix self-initialisation of ``X``/``Y``/``D`` in the Speckley
   ``DefaultAssembler2D``.

Documentation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - The ``run-escript`` man page now documents all launcher options (adds
   ``-a`` and ``-x``), corrects the ``-p``/``-f``/``-i``/``-o`` descriptions,
   and drops the untested ``-m`` (valgrind) option.

6.1
---
Highlights
~~~~~~~~~~
 - Trilinos has been upgraded from version 14 to **Trilinos 17.0.0** (bundled
   source built in-tree).  V14 source has been removed.
 - New public ``SolverFramework`` class replaces the ``SO_PACKAGE_PASO`` /
   ``SO_PACKAGE_TRILINOS`` constants and the ``setPackage`` API.  Domains can
   now be created with ``framework=SolverFramework.trilinos()`` (or ``.paso()``)
   to pin the linear-algebra back-end at construction time.
 - Major MPI-correctness fixes in Speckley, Ripley and the Trilinos wrapper
   (see below).  The test suite now runs cleanly under MPI > 1 ranks and
   under multi-thread OpenMP, where it previously aborted or silently produced
   corrupt matrices.
 - HDF5 dumping/loading support for complex Data, including mesh data.
 - InterpolationTable class for 1-D/2-D/3-D table-based interpolation,
   including complex-valued tables; replaces the deprecated
   ``Data.interpolateTable`` (issues #41, #55, #121).
 - macOS arm64 (Apple Silicon) build support, including Trilinos with MueLu
   and METIS via Homebrew.
 - GitHub Actions CI extended to test option templates across multiple Linux
   distributions and macOS.

Solvers and Trilinos integration
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - ``SolverFramework`` API; tests updated from ``setPackage`` to ``framework=``.
 - Trilinos 17.0.0 default; older 16.x source removed.
 - METIS / ParMETIS / Scotch / PT-Scotch as configurable dependencies; paths
   passed through to the Trilinos build.
 - Complex PDE solver defaults and compatibility fixes for Trilinos.
 - Fix Belos GMRES NaN bug for complex scalar types (issue #131).
 - Fix silent convergence failure in Belos solvers.
 - Fix PCG API: callers updated after return-value change, iteration history
   now optionally returned.
 - Fix MinimizerLBFGS restart mechanism and re-enable it (issue #105); alpha
   resets to ``initialAlpha`` on breakdown.

MPI correctness fixes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - Fix ``BlockCrsMatrixWrapper::nullifyRowsAndCols`` crash under MPI for
   system PDEs (e.g. LameEquation): the loop cast a local block-row index as
   a global ordinal, leading to ``std::vector(max_size())`` aborts or
   deadlocks at >1 ranks.
 - Templatise Speckley ``Brick`` neighbour-exchange functions to support
   complex Data.  Previously hard-coded ``double *`` / ``MPI_DOUBLE`` caused
   heap corruption (``corrupted double-linked list`` etc.) when complex
   coefficients were sent across rank boundaries.
 - Enable Nodes->Dirac and DOF->Dirac interpolation in Ripley under MPI.
 - Serialise ``Tpetra::CrsMatrix::sumIntoLocalValues`` in the Trilinos wrapper
   with ``#pragma omp critical``: previously a Kokkos
   ``SharedAllocationRecord`` race aborted Order-2 FEM assembly under
   OpenMP threading; the previously-skipped Order-2 ILU tests are
   re-enabled now that the underlying race is gone.

I/O and data handling
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - HDF5 dump/load for Data, including complex numbers and mesh data; netCDF
   dependency removed in favour of HDF5.
 - ``InterpolationTable`` (1-D/2-D/3-D, real or complex) with order-0 and
   order-1 interpolation; ``Data.interpolateTable`` deprecated.
 - Fix Python 3 ``zip()`` incompatibility in the Silo test comparator.
 - ``copyWithMask`` correctly handles a scalar mask on non-scalar data
   (issue #47).

Build system and platforms
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - macOS arm64 (Apple Silicon) support: Apple Clang + libomp, LLVM Clang +
   Python 3.14, ``--break-system-packages`` handling for pip on PEP 668
   systems, Homebrew packages preferred over pip, brew zlib/LAPACK/OpenBLAS
   wired up.
 - Option templates restructured: each platform has a base MPI variant and
   a ``_nompi`` variant.  Defaults switched ``build_trilinos`` from ``make``
   to ``check`` so partial builds reuse an existing Trilinos.
 - ``trilinos_cc`` / ``trilinos_cxx`` build options to override the compiler
   used for the Trilinos build (needed for MueLu on macOS arm64).
 - Linux distros covered in CI: Ubuntu, Debian, Mint, Sid, Arch, OpenSUSE,
   Rocky Linux.  Job timeouts raised to 120 minutes for Trilinos builds.
 - ``run-escript`` launcher fixes for macOS CPU detection and for Windows
   install paths containing spaces (issue #117).
 - The generated ``escript/py_src/run_escript.py`` is no longer source-
   controlled; it is regenerated by scons on every build from
   ``run_escript_py.in``.
 - C++20 destructor syntax fix in ``DataVectorAlt``.

Documentation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - User guide updated for the SolverFramework API.
 - Sphinx documentation restructured; user guide content linked from the
   landing page, examples ZIP/TAR.GZ archives generated alongside the API
   reference.
 - Third-party license notices added for Trilinos and p4est.
 - Installation instructions refreshed: Windows/WSL2 section, current Linux
   Mint instructions, python3-silo dependency noted, dynamic Python
   detection in option templates.
 - Copyright updated to 2026.

Test suite
~~~~~~~~~~
 - Test suite now passes cleanly under ``-t1 -n4 -p1`` (MPI), ``-t4 -n1 -p1``
   (OpenMP), and ``-t3 -n2 -p1`` (mixed).
 - Many MPI-rank-specific skips refined: 3D Multiresolution tests skip at
   ``mpiSize > 1`` (issue #132), odd-rank-count cases skip where Ripley's
   1xN decomposition exceeds a filter footprint, and the test_complex_table
   case uses ``Lsup`` instead of ``toListOfTuples`` so it is MPI-safe.

Removed
~~~~~~~
 - Trilinos 14 (replaced by Trilinos 17).
 - ``dudley`` module references; dudley itself was removed in 6.0.
 - ``splitworld`` references.
 - ``convert_latex.py`` and other obsolete scripts.
 - Demo / experiment scripts that previously sat in the repository root.

Known issues
~~~~~~~~~~~~
 - 3D Multiresolution domains (``MultiBrick``) do not yet support MPI;
   tests are skipped (issue #132).
 - ``run_savesilo_tests`` and ``run_savevtk_tests`` skip under MPI because
   their comparators assume single-block reference files; ``saveSilo``
   itself produces correct PMPIO output (issue #133).
 - Kokkos may report a finalize-time crash with Trilinos on shutdown;
   utest.sh treats exit code 134/139 as success when they occur after a
   successful run (issue #127).

6.0
---
 - escript now uses Trilinos 14.
 - escript now has a built-in Trilinos 14 compiler.
 - escript now utilises the p4est library.
 - The dudley module has been removed.
 - The oxley module has been added but is not fully functional yet.
 - sympy has been switched off.
 - packages downunder and models have been removed.

